(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["microbiology-exam-2-novel-antimicrobial-therapy-quiz"]={"title":"Novel Antimicrobial Therapy — Quiz 1","category":"Microbiology","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Why was phage therapy abandoned in the West after the 1920s and 1930s?","choices":["Antibiotic therapy emerged","Phages were found to infect humans","It was banned as too dangerous","Phages could not be grown"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: phage therapy was explored in eastern Europe in the 1920s and 1930s but abandoned in the West once antibiotics, such as penicillin in the 1940s, became available. — PAJ5200.Novel Antimicrobial Therapies-2.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"In a tailed bacteriophage, which structure holds the genome?","choices":["The baseplate","The long tail fibers","The head","The tail sheath"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the genome, shown as DNA, is packed in the head; the tail tube, sheath, baseplate and long tail fibers deliver it into the bacterium. — PAJ5200.Novel Antimicrobial Therapies-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which patient fits the high-risk group for which many dentists still give antibiotic prophylaxis before dental work?","choices":["A patient with well-controlled asthma","A patient with a prosthetic heart valve","A healthy patient having a routine cleaning","A patient with seasonal allergies"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: dental prophylaxis is now limited to a few high-risk scenarios, such as a prosthetic heart valve or a history of endocarditis. — PAJ5200.Novel Antimicrobial Therapies-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which Gram-positive organism is listed among the superbugs for its resistance to vancomycin?","choices":["Enterococcus","Lactobacillus","Streptococcus pneumoniae","Listeria monocytogenes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: vancomycin-resistant Enterococcus is on the superbug list, alongside vancomycin-resistant and vancomycin-intermediate Staphylococcus aureus. — PAJ5200.Novel Antimicrobial Therapies-2.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus is frequently resistant to ciprofloxacin and erythromycin. Which of these antibiotics typically remains active against it?","choices":["Ciprofloxacin","None of the listed drugs","Vancomycin","Erythromycin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: on a hospital antibiogram vancomycin kept full activity against methicillin-susceptible and methicillin-resistant strains alike, while ciprofloxacin and erythromycin susceptibility dropped sharply in the resistant ones. — PAJ5200.Novel Antimicrobial Therapies-2.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which statement about ear infections and antibiotics is accurate?","choices":["Every ear infection requires an antibiotic","Ear infections are never caused by bacteria","Ear infections respond only to antivirals","Some ear infections do not require an antibiotic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: some ear infections do not require an antibiotic, one of the six facts about antibiotic use alongside 'most sore throats do not'. — PAJ5200.Novel Antimicrobial Therapies-2.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Why are stool donors screened before fecal microbiota transplant?","choices":["To confirm the donor has taken antibiotics","To reduce the risk of passing on pathogens","To match the donor's blood type","To find donors who take probiotics"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: donor screening, similar to blood bank screening, reduces the risk of passing on blood-borne and other pathogens to the recipient. — PAJ5200.Novel Antimicrobial Therapies-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which is one of the hypotheses for why herbs and spices are used across cultures?","choices":["They sterilize food completely","Some kill bacteria and fungi in food","They replace salt as a preservative","They make food cheaper to produce"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: one hypothesis is that some herbs and spices kill bacteria and fungi, especially those that spoil food; others are taste, smell and nutrients. — PAJ5200.Novel Antimicrobial Therapies-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What material does a patient receive in a fecal microbiota transplant?","choices":["Screened donor stool","The patient's own blood plasma","A purified antibiotic","A suspension of bacteriophages"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: fecal microbiota transplant uses screened donor stool samples, often from stool banks, to restore the patient's gut microflora. — PAJ5200.Novel Antimicrobial Therapies-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A patient has had three recurrences of Clostridioides difficile infection despite treatment. Which novel therapy is established for preventing further recurrence?","choices":["Convalescent plasma","Fecal microbiota transplant","Systemic corticosteroids","Plant essential oils"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: fecal microbiota transplant has been specifically researched as a treatment for Clostridioides difficile infection, and preventing recurrence is its established use. — PAJ5200.Novel Antimicrobial Therapies-2.pptx, Slide 24 and 27"},{"q":"Which patients most commonly receive empiric antibiotic therapy?","choices":["Healthy patients with no symptoms","Patients whose cultures are already back","Only patients with viral infections","Critically ill or emergent patients"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: empiric therapy is common in human and veterinary medicine, especially for critically ill or emergent patients who cannot wait for results. — PAJ5200.Novel Antimicrobial Therapies-2.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which regulatory hurdle does phage therapy face in the United States?","choices":["Phages are classed as controlled substances","Phages are already fully approved for all infections","Phages may be sold only as dietary supplements","No phage product is yet approved as a medicine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: bacterial viruses are not yet established as approved medicinal products, so regulatory clearance is a concern; investigational drug status and emergency use authorization are routes around it. — PAJ5200.Novel Antimicrobial Therapies-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which foods are a common natural source of probiotics?","choices":["Fermented foods","Canned vegetables","Refined sugar","Pasteurized juice"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: probiotics such as Lactobacillus and Saccharomyces are often found in fermented foods. — PAJ5200.Novel Antimicrobial Therapies-2.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which injury is an example of a high-risk trauma for which antimicrobial prophylaxis is used?","choices":["A first-degree sunburn","A bruise from a fall","An animal bite","A closed ankle sprain"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: antimicrobial prophylaxis is used in traumatic injuries where the risk of infection is high, such as animal bites or penetrating brain injury. — PAJ5200.Novel Antimicrobial Therapies-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What still has to be precisely defined before phage therapy is used routinely?","choices":["The color of the phage preparation","Whether phages infect bacteria","Which patients prefer phages to pills","The dose, route, frequency and duration"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: phage therapy is not a magic bullet: the optimal dose, administration route, frequency and duration of treatment still need to be precisely defined. — PAJ5200.Novel Antimicrobial Therapies-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which cells do bacteriophages attack?","choices":["Only human cells","Bacteria and human cells alike","Fungi and parasites","Only bacteria"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: phages specifically attack bacteria and no other types of cells, which is why they are attractive as a therapy. — PAJ5200.Novel Antimicrobial Therapies-2.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Antimicrobial prophylaxis for close contacts is used to prevent the spread of which group of infections?","choices":["Tuberculosis, meningitis and pertussis","Influenza, measles and mumps","Tetanus, botulism and gas gangrene","Acne, cellulitis and impetigo"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: prophylaxis to prevent transmission of pathogens to susceptible contacts is used, for example, for tuberculosis, meningitis or pertussis. — PAJ5200.Novel Antimicrobial Therapies-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"In a disk diffusion (Kirby-Bauer) test, what does the clear zone around an antibiotic disk show?","choices":["Where the antibiotic was used up","Growth inhibited by the antibiotic","Bacteria that are resistant to the drug","Contamination from the paper disk"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the circular zone of inhibition is where the antibiotic diffusing from the paper disk stopped the bacteria growing, showing how effective that drug is. — PAJ5200.Novel Antimicrobial Therapies-2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Penicillin, vancomycin and azithromycin share which pattern?","choices":["Resistance took about a century to appear","Resistance appeared within years of widespread use","Resistance appeared before each drug was discovered","Resistance has never been detected to any of them"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: for each drug, resistance was first detected within a few years to about two decades of its widespread clinical use, a reminder that use selects for resistance. — PAJ5200.Novel Antimicrobial Therapies-2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"How does a prebiotic differ from a probiotic?","choices":["Prebiotics are antibiotics taken before meals","They are two names for the same thing","Prebiotics feed gut flora; probiotics are live microbes","Prebiotics are live microbes; probiotics feed them"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: prebiotics are fiber-rich foods that provide nutrients to the gut microflora; probiotics are the beneficial organisms themselves, such as Lactobacillus and Saccharomyces. — PAJ5200.Novel Antimicrobial Therapies-2.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What kind of genome can a bacteriophage carry?","choices":["Both DNA and RNA together","DNA only, never RNA","Protein only, with no nucleic acid","DNA or RNA, never both"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: like all viruses, bacteriophages can have DNA or RNA, but never both. — PAJ5200.Novel Antimicrobial Therapies-2.pptx, Slide 18 and 20"},{"q":"Why could phage therapy be more efficient than antibiotics once the infection is cleared?","choices":["Phages die off when their host bacteria are gone","Phages stay in the body for life","Phages become antibiotics in the body","Phages switch to attacking other bacteria"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: phages target specific bacterial cells and then die off when their host is no longer present, one reason given for greater efficacy than antibiotics. — PAJ5200.Novel Antimicrobial Therapies-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which plants are named among the herbs and spices studied for antimicrobial properties?","choices":["Garlic, oregano and cinnamon","Tobacco, coffee and tea","Wheat, rice and corn","Potato, carrot and celery"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the list includes garlic, onion, allspice, oregano, thyme, cinnamon, cumin, basil, ginger, cloves, rosemary and others. — PAJ5200.Novel Antimicrobial Therapies-2.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What is the main purpose of fecal microbiota transplant?","choices":["Delivering a high-dose antibiotic","Vaccinating against intestinal viruses","Removing all bacteria from the colon","Restoring a healthy gut microflora"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: fecal microbiota transplant gives screened donor stool to realign the patient's gastrointestinal microflora. — PAJ5200.Novel Antimicrobial Therapies-2.pptx, Slide 24 and 26"},{"q":"What is still needed before plant essential oils can be judged as antibacterial treatments?","choices":["Better experimental design","Nothing; they are already standard care","Only a lower price","Regulatory approval as antibiotics alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the hypotheses about herbs and spices need better experimental design to test them; in vitro activity alone does not show clinical benefit. — PAJ5200.Novel Antimicrobial Therapies-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which problem is the main force driving the search for novel antimicrobial therapies?","choices":["Antibiotics that cost too much","Viruses replacing bacteria as pathogens","Antimicrobial resistance","Shortages of antibiotic raw materials"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: resistance to antimicrobials and antibiotics heads the list of what drives the need for novel therapies, fed by overuse in agriculture, inappropriate prescribing and patients not finishing treatment. — PAJ5200.Novel Antimicrobial Therapies-2.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"An antibiotic does not clear a patient's infection. How is this classified among adverse effects of antimicrobials?","choices":["An indirect effect on the environment","It is not an adverse effect","A direct effect: therapeutic failure","An indirect effect on the gut flora"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: therapeutic failure is grouped with the direct adverse effects, alongside allergy, toxicity and drug-drug interactions. — PAJ5200.Novel Antimicrobial Therapies-2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which sexually transmitted pathogen is listed among the drug-resistant superbugs?","choices":["Herpes simplex virus","Neisseria gonorrhoeae","Treponema pallidum","Chlamydia trachomatis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: Neisseria gonorrhoeae is on the superbug list, with methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus, Mycobacterium tuberculosis and Pseudomonas. — PAJ5200.Novel Antimicrobial Therapies-2.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"'Yellow soup' from old Chinese texts is an early form of which therapy?","choices":["Convalescent plasma","Fecal microbiota transplant","Phage therapy","An herbal essential oil"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: recipes for 'yellow soup', from Ge Hong in the 4th century and Li Shizhen in the 16th, are early forms of fecal microbiota transplant. — PAJ5200.Novel Antimicrobial Therapies-2.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Clinically inappropriate antibiotic prescribing creates the need for which program?","choices":["Routine prophylaxis for every patient","Replacing all antibiotics with vaccines","Antimicrobial stewardship","Mandatory antibiotic rationing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: inappropriate prescriptions lead to the need for antimicrobial stewardship, which works through clinician and patient education. — PAJ5200.Novel Antimicrobial Therapies-2.pptx, Slide 3 and 4"}]};
